Figure 5.4 shows the number of constituents for jets with different transverse momenta and different resolution parameters. With the increase of resolution parameter the jet contains more tracks. For this reason the spectra for R = 0.4 are shifted to the right. In the analysis it was also decided to exclude the jets having only one constituent, which have no physical interest.

One of the problems arising during the jet reconstruction is large and fluctuating background from the underlying event with fluctuations of the magnitude of the signal. There are different methods which can be used for the background subtraction. One of them is the event-by-event correction, recommended by FastJet authors [40]. From the reconstructed jet transverse momentum, \(p_{\rm T,jet}^{\rm raw,ch}\) one needs to subtract the median jet energy density \(\rho\) multiplied by the jet area \(A\) as:

\[p_{\rm T,jet}^{\rm ch}=p_{\rm T,jet}^{\rm raw,ch}-\rho\cdot A. \tag{5.3}\]

The jet area \(A\) was calculated using the large amount of the extremely _ghost_ particles, which have been randomly added to the event. The median jet energy density is calculated as:

\[\rho={\rm med}\{\frac{p_{\rm T,jet}^{i}}{A_{i}}\} \tag{5.4}\]

for \(i\) running over all jets in the event. For the background calculation the \(k_{T}\) algorithm was used due to a better sensitivity to the soft particles.

Figure 5.5 shows the dependence of the reference multiplicity on the background density for R = 0.2 (left) and R = 0.4 (right). In Figure 5.6 the background energy density distributions for different centrality classes are presented for two resolution

